From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:37:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gl: compact_vertices, a batch without the vertex fields a
 program never reads (off by default)

A transformed draw's vertex shader reads the position's x, y and z, the texture coordinates and the colour, and the normal only when the
draw is lit; it never reads the position's w (gl_shaders.cpp uses it only for a through draw's texture rectangle). With
settings.compact_vertices (config.txt gl_compact_vertices, INFECTED_GL_COMPACT_VERTICES) such a draw is written to the batch as 36 bytes
(lit) or 24 (unlit) in place of the decoder's 40. The batch is now bytes: the stride comes from the program's flags
(vertex_stride_for), the vertex count is kept apart, and the attribute pointers are set again only when the stride changes between
batches (vitaGL starts a new vertex layout whenever one changes; a mission frame has about 14 such changes in 410 batches). A
through-mode draw keeps the whole vertex, its quads carry the texture rectangle in the normal and w. An attribute the program does not
read points at bytes inside the vertex. The copies are done in whole words (assume_aligned): a byte-aligned copy cost 23 ARM
instructions a vertex.

Measured on the mission-1 replay (100 frames, x86-64 software GL, MMPX time budget off so that a replay is repeatable):
 - pictures byte-identical with the switch off and on, with per-vertex and per-pixel lighting, and with --prepare --threaded;
 - vertex and index bytes a frame 890 KB -> 759 KB (-14.7 %). Three quarters of a mission frame's vertices are lit, so this is 15 %,
   not the third that an unlit-only layout would suggest;
 - the GL thread's ARM instructions a frame (armprof, --prepare) 2.498 M -> 2.537 M (+1.6 %);
 - framework tests 45 of 45; the desktop game builds.
Not verified: anything on the console (whether fewer bytes written and fetched shows in the frame time; how vitaGL treats a
three-component pointer for the vec4 position; the extra layout changes). The switch is off until it is.

Also: the previous commit's message gives the vertex as 36 bytes and the frame as 0.88 MB; it is 40 bytes and 0.96 MB
(20,480 x 40 + 68,550 x 2 = 956,300, the replay's own count).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/docs/GL_RENDERER.md b/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
index 0d899cd..fa4074b 100644
--- a/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
+++ b/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
@@ -115,6 +115,18 @@ to 3.0 million instructions a frame and the walk from 6.0 to 3.5 million. Every
   scratch buffer objects at the batch (`vglBufferData`) and draws, with no staging copy. The buffer objects and the
   attribute offsets stay bound from one batch to the next; blits, post passes and the overlay call `client_arrays()`
   first. On desktop GL the same class keeps one heap buffer and draws from client arrays.
+* **Compact vertices** (`compact_vertices`, off by default). The decoder's `Vertex` is 40 bytes (position with a w, normal,
+  texture coordinates, colour). A transformed draw's shader never reads the w, and an unlit one never reads the normal, so
+  with the switch on such a draw is written to the batch as 36 bytes (lit) or 24 (unlit): the batch is bytes, and its stride
+  comes from its program's flags (`vertex_stride_for`; every draw of a batch has the same flags). A through-mode draw keeps
+  the whole vertex, as its quads carry the texture rectangle in the normal and w. The attribute pointers are set again only
+  when the stride changes from one batch to the next (vitaGL starts a new vertex layout when a pointer changes; a mission
+  frame has about 14 such changes in 410 batches), and an attribute the program does not read points at bytes inside the
+  vertex. Checked on the mission-1 replay (100 frames): the pictures are identical byte for byte with the switch off and on,
+  with per-vertex and per-pixel lighting and with the split renderer; vertex and index bytes a frame go from 890 KB to
+  759 KB (three quarters of the vertices are lit, which is why it is 15 % and not a third); the GL thread's share of ARM
+  instructions a frame goes from 2.498 M to 2.537 M (the copies are done in whole words). **Not measured on the console:**
+  whether fewer bytes written and fetched shows in the frame time.
 * **The walker sends less**: lighting and material versions move only when a value changes (65 more merged draws a
   frame), and the split renderer takes a draw's vertex and index storage instead of copying it
   (`Renderer::submit_taking`).
diff --git a/host/gpu/gl/gl_renderer.cpp b/host/gpu/gl/gl_renderer.cpp
index 0d223c3..f683ca5 100644
--- a/host/gpu/gl/gl_renderer.cpp
+++ b/host/gpu/gl/gl_renderer.cpp
@@ -92,6 +92,21 @@ static_assert(offsetof(Vertex, position) == 0u && offsetof(Vertex, normal) == 16
                   offsetof(Vertex, texcoord) == 28u && offsetof(Vertex, color) == 36u,
